#
# [ -r ] FIRST, and never `if ! . file` or `. file || :`.
#
# In ash `.` is a special built-in: a file it cannot open ends the script
# outright, so neither an `||` nor an `if !` around it is ever reached. This
# daemon is a procd respawn service, so "ends outright" is a crash loop - of
# the one daemon that holds the join's single fallback reboot. The promise
# the old comment made here ("the fallback disarms itself instead, and says
# so") could not be kept: nothing was said, because nothing ran.
#
# /etc/init.d/bh-key-adopt already gets this right for boot-window.sh and
# says why. This is the same rule applied to the worker's own two sources.
if [ -r /usr/share/easymesh/addr.sh ]; then
	. /usr/share/easymesh/addr.sh
else
	logger -t bh-key-adopt "addr.sh will not load - the mesh address finisher is off"
fi
# Without join-health.sh there is no verdict, every node would look healthy,
# and the fuse would record "after reboot: healthy" for a box that is broken
# - a lie in the one record that survives. join_final_check() already treats
# a missing join_health_verdict as "disarmed"; this only makes sure we are
# still alive to reach it, and that the reason is on the record.
if [ -r /usr/share/easymesh/join-health.sh ]; then
	. /usr/share/easymesh/join-health.sh
else
	logger -t bh-key-adopt "join-health.sh will not load - fallback reboot disarmed"
fi
#
# /usr/sbin/bh-key-adopt - turn credentials the mesh delivered into a backhaul.
#
# A joining node gets its credentials over the wire: the WSC exchange writes
# them into the mapagent ap sections. That is where the chain used to end -
# nothing built the STA side from them, because the only code that creates
# bsta sections (map_genconfig) is gated on a U-Boot variable no image ever
# sets. The first from-scratch join (2026-08-10) stalled exactly there: keys
# delivered, node with no way to use them.
#
# So this daemon watches for that moment. When the mapagent backhaul section
# carries a key and the MLD STA either does not exist or carries a different
# one, it builds both backhaul sides with the delivered key, turns the radios
# back on (the join left them dark on purpose - a keyless box has nothing
# safe to put on the air), sweeps the legacy BSSes that would steal the MLD's
# slots, and reboots. Reboot, not wifi reload: a runtime reload assigns
# chanctx wrongly on MT7996 - mlo-backhaul-setup says so on its own doorstep.
#
# Converged is the steady state: sta_mld_1 exists and its key matches the
# delivered one, so after the one reboot this loop never acts again - until a
# key rotation makes the same drift reappear, which is then handled the same
# way. The attempt counter is the reboot-loop fuse: if adoption does not
# converge, it goes loud and stops instead of power-cycling the node forever.

INTERVAL=30
MARK=/etc/mapc/bh-adopt.count
MAX_TRIES=3

# The backhaul network's name. Both readers below match on it, so a backhaul
# rename touches one line instead of two that can drift apart.
#
# MAP--BH is our mesh's. Under a controller that is not ours the backhaul is
# whatever that controller calls it (MediaTek R3: Multi-AP-1), and the name
# arrives with the key. It is kept in /etc/mapc/bh-ssid and read on every
# pass, because it can change while this loop runs: a WPS join to a foreign
# controller writes it a moment before the key is adopted.
BH_SSID_FILE=/etc/mapc/bh-ssid
bh_ssid_name() {
	[ -s "$BH_SSID_FILE" ] && { cat "$BH_SSID_FILE"; return; }
	echo MAP--BH
}
BH_SSID=$(bh_ssid_name)

log() { logger -t bh-key-adopt "$1"; }

# Reboot, or the part of a boot that matters here without one.
#
# The join restarts the box to raise what it has just written: the MLD
# backhaul, a delivered fronthaul, a mesh address. easymesh-soft-restart
# replays that part of the boot - service order, the radios' hotplug, network
# reload - and was measured against the reboot phase by phase (2026-09-19).
# This is now the default. It proved itself on hardware: 10/10 rounds on
# corridor (2026-09-19) and then four joins from a factory state on three
# different 4 GB boxes plus corridor (2026-09-20), none of which restarted
# once, where the same join used to cost four reboots and some eight minutes.
#
# The way back is an opt-OUT: /etc/mapc/join-with-reboot restores the old
# path. A box that gets into trouble can be put back to the behaviour we have
# shipped for months by touching one file, without a downgrade.
#
# The old marker is still honoured, because boxes in the field carry it and
# taking it away would silently change what they do.
# Returns only on the soft path; the reboot does not return.
JOIN_WITH_REBOOT=/etc/mapc/join-with-reboot
# There is no join-without-reboot file to look for: not rebooting is what
# happens unless JOIN_WITH_REBOOT asks otherwise. The name outlived the
# opt-in it belonged to (soft join became the default 2026-09-20) and then
# cost an afternoon on 2026-09-21, when a stale box still carried the file
# and it read as the switch that decides this.
soft_join() {
	[ -f "$JOIN_WITH_REBOOT" ] && return 1
	[ -x /usr/sbin/easymesh-soft-restart ]
}

# The way back from a join that ended half-done.
#
# A join without reboots has no restart left to hide behind: whatever it
# failed to raise stays down, quietly, and the node looks joined. So the join
# gets one last look at itself, and one reboot to spend if that look is bad.
#
# One. Not a retry loop - if a reboot did not fix it, that is a finding, and
# a second one only buys a longer outage and a worse record of what happened.
#
# The fuse is the record, not the permission slip: it is written at the moment
# the box is actually going down, and the line that says how it turned out is
# appended after it comes back. A fuse burned before the work would switch
# this off forever while reading as done - measured 2026-09-04, a box kept its
# factory address with a marker in place saying it had been given one.
FALLBACK=/etc/mapc/join-fallback-reboot
JOIN_STARTED=/etc/mapc/join-started
JOIN_DEADLINE=600

# Only the soft path. The reboot-driven join already restarts three times and
# has never needed a net; adding one there would change a path that works.
fallback_armed() { soft_join; }

now_iso() { date -Is 2>/dev/null || date; }

# One real reboot at the end of a soft join.
#
# The soft join replays userspace only: services, `wifi down/up`, a synthetic
# ieee80211 hotplug. The kernel, mt76 and the MT7996 firmware keep whatever the
# join left in them - the temporary non-MLO station on radio0, the factory
# non-MLO APs, several teardowns of the MLO interfaces. Measured 2026-09-29/30:
# a box straight after a soft join lost its uplink in the parent ("zombie")
# 6-74 min after every association, 7 times in one evening and 3 times on
# 30. 9. (twice ten minutes after association); after its first reboot, 0 in
# 5.5 h and ~14 associations. One snapshot showed the bSTA vif queue bound to
# wcid 0 instead of the link's wcid, a state no rebooted box ever had. The
# cause is not found; a reboot is the one step known to leave the driver clean.
#
# So the join ends where a boot ends: once, after the node has settled and
# passed its final check. The record is written before going down and
# completed after coming back, like the fallback fuse; a reboot that does not
# happen is said, never retried.
CLEAN=/etc/mapc/join-clean-reboot

# The outcome, written on the round after the reboot. Never reboots: a
# settled node reaches this every interval, for ever.
join_clean_outcome() {
	[ -s "$CLEAN" ] || return 0
	grep -q " done$\| did not take" "$CLEAN" 2>/dev/null && return 0
	if grep -q "boot=$(boot_id)" "$CLEAN" 2>/dev/null; then
		printf '%s did not take (same boot)\n' "$(now_iso)" >> "$CLEAN"
		log "clean reboot after the join did not take - not trying again"
	else
		printf '%s done\n' "$(now_iso)" >> "$CLEAN"
		log "clean reboot after the join done"
	fi
	sync
}

# The reboot, on the way IN to settled only (one join, one call).
join_clean_reboot() {
	soft_join || return 0
	# A join that already spent its fallback reboot has been through a boot.
	grep -q "after reboot:" "$FALLBACK" 2>/dev/null && return 0
	printf '%s reboot after join boot=%s\n' "$(now_iso)" "$(boot_id)" > "$CLEAN"
	sync
	grep -q "boot=$(boot_id)" "$CLEAN" 2>/dev/null || {
		log "could not write $CLEAN - not rebooting"
		return 0
	}
	log "join settled - one reboot to leave the driver in a clean state"
	sleep 5
	reboot
	sleep 120
	log "clean reboot after the join did not take"
}

# This box, this boot. The fuse says which boot decided to reboot, so the
# line that reports the outcome can prove a reboot actually happened rather
# than assuming it did - `reboot` can fail to take, and an outcome written
# after a reboot that never occurred is a fabricated record.
boot_id() { cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id 2>/dev/null; }

# The last word of the join. Returns on every path that does not reboot.
join_final_check() {
	local why="$1" bad

	# Nothing left to watch for means the STAMP goes, not only the check.
	#
	# 778fbe4 closed the healthy path; these two stayed. They return 0 without
	# touching JOIN_STARTED, so on a box carrying the reboot opt-out (or with
	# the health rules missing) the stamp outlives the join - and both callers
	# create it with `[ -s ] ||`, so the NEXT join on that box inherits it,
	# starts past a deadline meant to give it ten minutes, and has its one
	# fallback reboot available to spend in the first second of it.
	#
	# The rule is the one the temporary-fuse lesson states: a fuse ends in a
	# STATE ("this join is over"), never in a deadline - `age >= DEADLINE` is
	# true for ever once it is true at all.
	fallback_armed || { rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"; return 0; }
	command -v join_health_verdict >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
		{ rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"; return 0; }

	# Before the looking, not after: the verdict costs 20 s, and once the
	# fuse is spent there is nothing left to decide. Paying it every 30 s
	# round would slow down the adopt work below by 40 % for ever.
	if [ -s "$FALLBACK" ] && grep -q "after reboot:" "$FALLBACK" 2>/dev/null; then
		rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"
		# The verdict after the reboot is already on record. If it was bad,
		# this node is failed and stays failed: saying "settled" a moment
		# later would hide the one join the fallback could not repair.
		grep -q "after reboot: still broken" "$FALLBACK" 2>/dev/null && return 1
		return 0
	fi

	join_health_verdict final
	bad="$JOIN_HEALTH_BAD"

	# A fuse without an outcome means this is the round after the reboot:
	# say what it bought, whichever way it went, and never reboot again.
	if [ -s "$FALLBACK" ]; then
		if grep -q "boot=$(boot_id)" "$FALLBACK" 2>/dev/null; then
			# Same boot as the one that wrote the fuse, so the reboot
			# did not happen. Say that, and do not call it an outcome.
			#
			# The fuse stays open so the outcome can still be written if
			# the box does go down later. The stamp does not: this join is
			# over whichever way it ended, and leaving it behind hands the
			# next join on this box a deadline that is already past.
			log "fallback reboot did not take (same boot) - leaving the fuse open"
			rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"
			return 0
		fi
		if [ -n "$bad" ]; then
			printf '%s after reboot: still broken:%s (looks:%s)\n' \
				"$(now_iso)" "$bad" "$JOIN_HEALTH_LOOKS" >> "$FALLBACK"
			log "the fallback reboot did not finish the join:$bad"
			state failed
			sync
			rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"
			return 1
		else
			printf '%s after reboot: healthy\n' "$(now_iso)" >> "$FALLBACK"
			log "the fallback reboot finished the join"
		fi
		sync
		rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"
		return 0
	fi

	# The join is over and it came out good, so the stamp has to go.
	#
	# It used to stay, and that is worse than it looks: easymesh-wps-join
	# and easymesh both create it with `[ -s ... ] ||`, so they stamp only
	# when there is no stamp. A leftover one is therefore INHERITED by the
	# next join on this box, which then starts life already past a deadline
	# meant to give it ten minutes. Measured 2026-09-21: three of four nodes
	# were carrying a stamp 13 hours old, so their next join would have had
	# no grace at all and could have spent its one fallback reboot in the
	# first second of it.
	[ -z "$bad" ] && { rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"; return 0; }

	# Write first, prove the write, THEN go down. An overlay that is full or
	# read-only would otherwise leave an empty file, which reads as "no fuse"
	# - and the box would reboot again every ten minutes, for ever.
	log "join incomplete at $why:$bad - spending the one fallback reboot"
	printf '%s reboot because (%s):%s boot=%s (looks:%s)\n' \
		"$(now_iso)" "$why" "$bad" "$(boot_id)" "$JOIN_HEALTH_LOOKS" >> "$FALLBACK"
	sync
	if ! grep -q "reboot because" "$FALLBACK" 2>/dev/null; then
		log "could not write the fuse - not rebooting"
		return 0
	fi
	reboot
	sleep 120
	log "reboot did not take"
}

# Has the join been going too long to still be going?
#
# Measured in wall clock, because it has to survive the reboot that resets
# uptime - and guarded, because a box that has just found NTP moves its clock
# under us. A stamp in the future, or a day old, is not a measurement; it is
# a clock that jumped, so the stamp is reset and the count starts again.
join_overdue() {
	local started now age
	[ -s "$JOIN_STARTED" ] || return 1
	started=$(cat "$JOIN_STARTED" 2>/dev/null)
	now=$(date +%s 2>/dev/null)
	[ -n "$now" ] || return 1
	# Validate BEFORE the arithmetic, not after: in ash a non-numeric word
	# inside $(( )) is read as a variable name (so "abc" quietly becomes 0),
	# and something like "1 2" is a hard error that kills the whole daemon.
	# `x 2>/dev/null || return 1` on an assignment catches neither.
	case "$started" in
		''|*[!0-9]*) echo "$now" > "$JOIN_STARTED"; return 1 ;;
	esac
	age=$((now - started))
	if [ "$age" -lt 0 ] || [ "$age" -gt 86400 ]; then
		echo "$now" > "$JOIN_STARTED"
		log "join stamp is $age s away from now - the clock moved, restarting the count"
		return 1
	fi
	[ "$age" -ge "$JOIN_DEADLINE" ] 2>/dev/null
}

restart_to_apply() {
	if soft_join; then
		log "$1 - soft restart instead of a reboot"
		/usr/sbin/easymesh-soft-restart "bh-key-adopt: $1" || true
		return 0
	fi
	log "$1 - rebooting"
	sync; reboot
	# reboot returns at once and the box goes down behind it; nothing
	# after this line should do work.
	sleep 60
}

# One word for the setup overlay: working | settled | failed. setup_state
# publishes it over the null rpcd session, which is the only channel that
# answers while the box is rebooting and no sysauth session exists.
#
# It lives on the rootfs, not in /tmp, and that is the point: right after a
# reboot the loop has not run yet (it sleeps its full interval first), and a
# state that vanished with the reboot would read as "nothing to wait for" at
# exactly the moment there is. Measured 2026-08-15: an over-the-air join takes
# THREE restarts - role, join, finisher - and the wizard narrated two. The gap
# between the second and the third is the dangerous one: the login screen is
# back, so the user signs in and starts working, and then the box disappears
# under their hands and reads as dead rather than as busy.
STATE=/etc/mapc/adopt-state
state() {
	mkdir -p /etc/mapc 2>/dev/null
	[ "$(cat $STATE 2>/dev/null)" = "$1" ] && return 0
	echo "$1" > $STATE 2>/dev/null
	# The lamp says "joining" from the button press on, and nothing ever
	# said "done": a reboot used to clear it by accident. Without reboots it
	# blinked on for good (2026-09-19). Answer it where the join ends.
	[ -x /usr/sbin/easymesh-led ] || return 0
	case "$1" in
	settled) /usr/sbin/easymesh-led ok >/dev/null 2>&1 ;;
	failed)  /usr/sbin/easymesh-led refused >/dev/null 2>&1 ;;
	esac
}

# Raise a fronthaul that has just been written, without spending a restart -
# and say honestly whether it worked.
#
# The restart is here because it is reliable, and that is worth more than the
# time it costs. But measured 2026-08-25 on the controller, 20 times out of 20:
# changing the fronthaul ssid and key on an existing MLD section and reloading
# put the new name on the air in 7 s with every link present. The operation
# that is NOT safe is a different one - adding a scanning STA beside a running
# MLD took a link away 9 times in 30 and never gave it back. This one only
# rewrites sections that already exist.
#
# So try the cheap way and keep the reliable one behind it. Success is not
# "the interface came back": it is the air carrying a name AND the AP-MLD
# holding every link it was built from. A reload that returns a link short
# leaves the node broadcasting on fewer bands than it should, silently, and
# that is worse than a restart nobody minds. When in any doubt, return 1 and
# let the caller reboot.
fh_raise_by_reload() {
	fr_if=$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.ifname)
	[ -n "$fr_if" ] || return 1

	# How many links this MLD was built from. A radio held down in config is
	# not expected to carry one.
	fr_want=0
	for fr_r in $(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.device); do
		[ "$(uci -q get wireless.$fr_r.disabled)" = "1" ] || fr_want=$((fr_want + 1))
	done
	[ "$fr_want" -gt 0 ] || return 1

	wifi down >/dev/null 2>&1
	sleep 4
	wifi up >/dev/null 2>&1

	fr_n=0
	while [ $fr_n -lt 60 ]; do
		fr_air=$(iw dev "$fr_if" info 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/^[[:space:]]*ssid //p')
		fr_have=$(iw dev "$fr_if" info 2>/dev/null | grep -c '^[[:space:]]*channel')
		[ -n "$fr_air" ] && [ "$fr_have" -ge "$fr_want" ] && {
			log "reload raised $fr_if with $fr_have/$fr_want links"
			return 0
		}
		fr_n=$((fr_n + 1))
		sleep 1
	done
	log "reload left $fr_if with $(iw dev "$fr_if" info 2>/dev/null | grep -c '^[[:space:]]*channel')/$fr_want links after 60 s"
	return 1
}

delivered_bh_key() {
	# Match the SSID, not just the type - and never the position.
	#
	# uci addresses @ap[N] by order of appearance, and the order is a
	# genconfig implementation detail. Measured 2026-08-15 on the BPI-R4 Pro 8X: the
	# bhmld_ap_5/6 sections came out FIRST there (they sit last, and empty,
	# on the classic boards) and carried the fronthaul credential under
	# type=backhaul. Matching on type alone therefore handed back the
	# fronthaul key, which never equals wireless.sta_mld_1.key - so the node
	# re-adopted a backhaul it already had until the fuse blew, and the
	# finisher below, which lives in the converged branch, never ran once.
	# The fronthaul stayed on the rescue SSID and the legacy BSSes it left
	# standing took radio1+radio2 out from under the backhaul.
	#
	# wps_delivered_key() already matched on the name. This is the same rule
	# applied to the door the wire comes in through.
	local i=0 t s k
	while uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i] >/dev/null 2>&1; do
		t=$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].type)
		s=$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].ssid)
		k=$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].key)
		if [ "$t" = "backhaul" ] && [ "$s" = "$BH_SSID" ] && [ -n "$k" ]; then
			echo "$k"
			return 0
		fi
		i=$((i + 1))
	done
	return 1
}

# radios_on - being on is a condition this daemon holds, not a side effect.
#
# adopt() is what deletes wireless.radioN.disabled, and there is a path that
# never reaches adopt(): when the key the mesh delivers already equals the one
# in the config - every re-registration, and every node that kept its config
# across the join - the converged branch runs instead. easymesh-role turned the
# radios off on its way out, nothing turned them back on, and the node sat dark
# while adopt-state said "settled". Measured 2026-08-19 on bpi-r4-sd, and it is
# the same silence that ended 2026-08-18.
radios_on() {
	local r changed=0
	for r in radio0 radio1 radio2; do
		uci -q get wireless.$r >/dev/null 2>&1 || continue
		[ -n "$(uci -q get wireless.$r.disabled)" ] || continue
		uci -q delete wireless.$r.disabled
		changed=1
	done
	[ "$changed" = "1" ] && {
		uci commit wireless
		log "radios were dark with the backhaul already in place - turning them back on"
	}

	# The config saying "on" is not the same as radios being on, and only the
	# second one carries traffic. Measured 2026-08-19: the flag was cleared and
	# committed, `wifi reload` was launched detached from a service that apk
	# was in the middle of restarting, and the box came up with an empty
	# `iw dev` - no wireless interfaces at all - while every uci value said the
	# radios were enabled. It took `wifi up` by hand.
	#
	# So the state to hold is the interface list, not the flag. `up` and not
	# `reload`: reload reconfigures what exists, and here nothing does.
	iw dev 2>/dev/null | grep -q Interface && return 0

	# Not more than once every five minutes. Repeated wifi restarts are how
	# the MT7996 MCU gets wedged, and a box that needs this twice in a row has
	# a different problem that another reload will not solve.
	#
	# The stamp is uptime, so it belongs in /tmp and nowhere else. Written to
	# /etc it survives the reboot that resets the clock it is measured in: the
	# file then holds a number larger than "now", the difference comes out
	# negative, negative is less than 300, and the one thing this function
	# exists to do never happens again. Caught before it shipped, on the test
	# that would have measured it instead of the fix.
	local now last
	now=$(cut -d. -f1 /proc/uptime 2>/dev/null)
	last=$(cat /tmp/radios-up.at 2>/dev/null)
	[ -n "$last" ] && [ "$last" -le "$now" ] 2>/dev/null &&
		[ $((now - last)) -lt 300 ] 2>/dev/null && return 0
	echo "$now" > /tmp/radios-up.at

	log "radios enabled in config but no wireless interfaces exist - bringing them up"
	# setsid so it outlives a service restart that may be what got us here.
	if command -v setsid >/dev/null 2>&1; then
		setsid wifi up >/dev/null 2>&1 &
	else
		wifi up >/dev/null 2>&1 &
	fi
}

adopt() {
	local key="$1" tries fp prev

	# Count attempts PER KEY, not per node.
	#
	# Every attempt ends in a reboot, so a plain counter spends itself in
	# three restarts and then locks the node out of adoption for good -
	# including from settings that arrive afterwards and would have worked.
	# Measured 2026-08-15 on a from-scratch BPI-R4 Pro 8X join: the early attempts
	# ran while mapagent still carried an older backhaul key, the right one
	# was delivered a little later, and by then the fuse was spent. The node
	# sat with a correct key in mapagent, a wrong one in wireless, and a
	# daemon that refused to look. Clearing the counter by hand converged it
	# on the first pass.
	#
	# A different delivered key is a different situation, not another go at
	# the same one, so it starts its own count. The fuse still stops a real
	# loop - the same key failing three times - which is all it was for.
	fp=$(printf %s "$key" | md5sum | cut -c1-12)
	prev=$(cat "$MARK" 2>/dev/null)
	case "$prev" in
		"$fp":*) tries=${prev#*:} ;;
		*)       tries=0; rm -f /etc/mapc/bh-adopt.loud ;;
	esac
	[ "$tries" -ge 0 ] 2>/dev/null || tries=0
	if [ "$tries" -ge "$MAX_TRIES" ] 2>/dev/null; then
		# Once, not once every INTERVAL seconds forever. The blown fuse is
		# a standing condition, not an event; on 2026-08-15 it wrote the
		# same sentence into the log every 30 s for over an hour.
		state failed
		[ -f /etc/mapc/bh-adopt.loud ] || {
			: > /etc/mapc/bh-adopt.loud
			log "gave up after $tries attempts - backhaul config does not converge, leaving the node alone"
		}
		return 1
	fi
	mkdir -p /etc/mapc 2>/dev/null
	echo "$fp:$((tries + 1))" > "$MARK"
	state working

	log "mesh delivered a backhaul key - building the MLD backhaul from it (attempt $((tries + 1)))"

	# Radios first, sections second - the order is load-bearing. The tool's
	# emitters skip every radio that is missing or disabled, so applied
	# against the dark radios the join left behind they write a STA section
	# with an empty device list, which netifd silently ignores: a backhaul
	# that exists in uci and nowhere else. Cost of learning this: three
	# reboots and a powercycle on 2026-08-10.
	local r
	for r in radio0 radio1 radio2; do
		uci -q get wireless.$r >/dev/null 2>&1 && uci -q delete wireless.$r.disabled
	done
	uci commit wireless

	/usr/sbin/mlo-backhaul-setup --apply-ap --key "$key" --ssid "$BH_SSID" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
		{ log "apply-ap failed"; return 1; }
	/usr/sbin/mlo-backhaul-setup --apply-sta --key "$key" --ssid "$BH_SSID" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
		{ log "apply-sta failed"; return 1; }
	[ -r /usr/share/easymesh/rpc.sh ] && { . /usr/share/easymesh/rpc.sh; mesh_rpc_user; }

	[ -x /usr/sbin/drop-legacy-fronthaul ] && /usr/sbin/drop-legacy-fronthaul

	# Run the canonical writer here, where the settings are known to exist.
	#
	# easymesh-genconfig owns /etc/config/wireless: it reconciles every MLD
	# section from the mapagent ap sections, fronthaul included. At boot it
	# runs from S19 - before map-agent has been told anything by the
	# controller - so it writes whatever the config happens to hold at that
	# moment and calls it done. Measured 2026-08-15 on a from-scratch BPI-R4 Pro 8X
	# join: three boots in a row it wrote a backhaul key that was not the
	# delivered one and left the fronthaul on the rescue SSID, this daemon
	# corrected it, rebooted, and S19 undid the correction again. Running
	# genconfig by hand once the credentials had arrived fixed both values in
	# a single pass.
	#
	# So the writer runs on the event rather than on the boot order: the
	# moment adoption has a delivered key is exactly the moment there is
	# something true to write. The apply steps above create the sections;
	# genconfig only updates sections that already exist, so it has to follow
	# them, not lead.
	[ -x /usr/sbin/easymesh-genconfig ] && /usr/sbin/easymesh-genconfig

	restart_to_apply "backhaul built, radios on"
	return 0
}

# The wire is no longer the only messenger: a WPS push-button join
# (wps_pbc with multi_ap=1 against an armed fronthaul) delivers the
# backhaul credential as a wps_credentials ubus event instead of a WSC
# write into the mapagent config. Spool those events and lift the key
# into the same mapagent sections the wire would have written - one
# adoption path, two doors in. Proven on hardware 2026-08-11.
WPS_SPOOL=/tmp/bh-adopt-wps.log
ubus listen wps_credentials > "$WPS_SPOOL" 2>/dev/null &
WPS_PID=$!
trap '[ -n "$WPS_PID" ] && kill "$WPS_PID" 2>/dev/null' EXIT

wps_delivered_key() {
	# Only the backhaul credential matters here; the fronthaul one from a
	# plain (no multi_ap) WPS round must not build a backhaul.
	#
	# Which one it is used to be told by the name: MAP--BH or nothing. That
	# held while the only registrar was our own controller. A foreign one
	# names its backhaul itself (MediaTek R3: Multi-AP-1), and its credential
	# was thrown away here (2026-09-18). So the rule is now who ASKED: our
	# station interface only ever runs WPS with multi_ap=1, and a Multi-AP
	# registrar answers that with the backhaul credential, whatever it is
	# called. MAP--BH still wins when both are in the spool.
	# Prints "<ssid> <key>"; the SSID carries no spaces in the WPS event we
	# read, and a name with spaces is refused rather than mangled.
	local line key ssid
	[ -s "$WPS_SPOOL" ] || return 1
	line=$(grep "\"ssid\":\"MAP--BH\"" "$WPS_SPOOL" | tail -1)
	[ -n "$line" ] || line=$(grep "\"ifname\":\"[^\"]*sta" "$WPS_SPOOL" | tail -1)
	[ -n "$line" ] || return 1
	ssid=$(echo "$line" | sed -n "s/.*\"ssid\":\"\([^\"]*\)\".*/\1/p")
	key=$(echo "$line" | sed -n "s/.*\"key\":\"\([^\"]*\)\".*/\1/p")
	[ -n "$ssid" ] && [ -n "$key" ] || return 1
	case "$ssid" in *" "*) return 1 ;; esac
	echo "$ssid $key"
}

while :; do
	sleep "$INTERVAL"

	# The controller is handed its key at setup; this path is for the nodes
	# that have to be told theirs.
	[ "$(uci -q get mapcontroller.controller.enabled)" = "1" ] && continue

	# A join that never reaches its end never reaches the check at the end
	# of it either - a fronthaul that is not sent, a controller that went
	# away mid-join. Ten minutes after the button is long enough that the
	# slowest good round (7.8 min, 2026-09-19) is not disturbed, and short
	# enough that somebody standing at the box has not yet given up.
	if [ "$(cat $STATE 2>/dev/null)" != settled ] && join_overdue; then
		join_final_check "10 minutes after the button"
	fi

	# A WPS-delivered key lands in the spool first; stage it where the
	# wired path would have written it, then the normal flow below takes
	# over (including the radios-first ordering the adopt step owns).
	wcred=$(wps_delivered_key) && [ -n "$wcred" ] && {
		wssid=${wcred%% *}
		wkey=${wcred#* }
		mkdir -p /etc/mapc
		echo "$wssid" > "$BH_SSID_FILE"
		BH_SSID=$wssid
		i=0
		while uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i] >/dev/null 2>&1; do
			[ "$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].type)" = "backhaul" ] && {
				uci set mapagent.@ap[$i].ssid="$wssid"
				uci set mapagent.@ap[$i].key="$wkey"
			}
			i=$((i + 1))
		done
		uci commit mapagent
		: > "$WPS_SPOOL"
		log "WPS delivered a backhaul credential for '$wssid' - staged into mapagent config"
		# The boxes log in to each other with a password made from this key.
		[ -r /usr/share/easymesh/rpc.sh ] && { . /usr/share/easymesh/rpc.sh; mesh_rpc_user; }
	}

	# The name can have been changed by someone else meanwhile (wps-join).
	BH_SSID=$(bh_ssid_name)

	# A WPS join in progress has not delivered anything yet. The key in the
	# mapagent sections at this point is the one `multiap conf` generated for
	# this box itself; adopting it rebuilt the radios for nothing - a reboot
	# (or a 2 min soft restart) before every WPS, and it took the join's own
	# station off the air while the controller's WPS window was open
	# (2026-09-19). wps-join removes the note once the real credential is
	# written, and the next pass adopts that one.
	if [ -f /etc/easymesh-wps-pending ]; then
		# A note with nobody behind it is worse than no note at all:
		# easymesh-genconfig exits at its first line, the button answers
		# "press the controller instead", and phase 1 has already deleted
		# every wifi-iface. The box then has no radios, no config writer
		# and no button, and the only way back is the ten second wipe.
		#
		# Twenty minutes is past the slowest good join measured (7.8 min,
		# 2026-09-19) with room for the band walk's restarts on top.
		_st=$(awk '{print $3}' /etc/easymesh-wps-pending 2>/dev/null)
		case "$_st" in ''|*[!0-9]*) _st="" ;; esac
		if [ -n "$_st" ] && [ $(($(date +%s) - _st)) -gt 1200 ] &&
		   ! grep -lq easymesh-wps-join /proc/[0-9]*/cmdline 2>/dev/null; then
			log "a WPS join note has been lying about for 20 min with no worker - clearing it"
			rm -f /etc/easymesh-wps-pending /etc/mapc/wps-join-band
			echo "error: the join was abandoned - press the button again" \
				> /tmp/wps-join.state
			state failed
		fi
		continue
	fi

	key=$(delivered_bh_key) || continue

	# Converged means the STA carries what WE build, not only the key.
	#
	# OpenWrt's own WPS handler writes the credential straight into the
	# station section - SSID, key and the encryption the registrar named.
	# A MediaTek registrar names WPA2-PSK, which an MLO station cannot use
	# (the supplicant drops it right after association). The key then
	# matched and this branch called it done, so mlo-backhaul-setup, which
	# writes sae and the right name on both sides, never ran (2026-09-18).
	cur=$(uci -q get wireless.sta_mld_1.key)
	[ "$(uci -q get wireless.sta_mld_1.ssid)" = "$BH_SSID" ] &&
		[ "$(uci -q get wireless.sta_mld_1.encryption)" = "sae" ] || cur=""
	if [ "$cur" = "$key" ]; then
		# The backhaul is in place, so whatever else is true, the radios
		# have no reason to be off.
		radios_on
		# Converged. Clear the fuse so a future rotation starts fresh.
		[ -f "$MARK" ] && rm -f "$MARK"
		[ -f /etc/mapc/bh-adopt.loud ] && rm -f /etc/mapc/bh-adopt.loud
		# Backhaul is done - but the fronthaul name arrives one step
		# later. On an over-the-air join the credential comes by WPS
		# for the backhaul only; the mesh SSID (the fronthaul one) is handed over by
		# WSC M2 *after* the node has associated, i.e. after the boot
		# where genconfig already ran. So this is the finisher: once
		# the backhaul stands, if the fronthaul the controller now
		# advertises does not match what this box broadcasts, run the
		# one writer and, if it actually changed the air, reboot once
		# to raise it - the second restart the user is warned about.
		# The fuse remembers WHICH credential it settled on, not merely
		# that it fired once. A rename or a password change later in the
		# node's life is a new value and has to be finished the same way;
		# the same value twice still cannot loop.
		#
		# Measured 2026-08-24: the fuse was an empty file written at the
		# first join, and the promise above - that a rotation clears it -
		# was never kept, because the converged path clears $MARK and
		# bh-adopt.loud and not this one. So every rename after the join
		# was swallowed: the controller sent the renew, map-agent stored
		# the new name within five seconds, and the air kept the old one
		# for as long as the box stayed up. Two renames were measured on
		# one afternoon, neither reached a radio without a hand-made reboot.
		fh=""; fhk=""
		i=0
		while uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i] >/dev/null 2>&1; do
			[ "$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].type)" = "fronthaul" ] &&
				[ -n "$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].ssid)" ] &&
				{ fh="$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].ssid)"
				  fhk="$(uci -q get mapagent.@ap[$i].key)"; break; }
			i=$((i + 1))
		done
		want="$fh|$fhk"
		if [ -n "$fh" ] &&
		   { [ "$fh" != "$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.ssid)" ] ||
		     [ "$fhk" != "$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.key)" ]; } &&
		   [ "$want" != "$(cat /etc/mapc/fh-finish.done 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
			mkdir -p /etc/mapc
			before="$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.ssid)|$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.key)"
			/usr/sbin/easymesh-genconfig
			if [ "$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.ssid)|$(uci -q get wireless.ap_mld_1.key)" != "$before" ]; then
				printf '%s\n' "$want" > /etc/mapc/fh-finish.done
				sync
				if fh_raise_by_reload; then
					log "fronthaul settled to '$fh' - raised without a restart"
					continue
				fi
				state working
				restart_to_apply "fronthaul settled to '$fh', the reload did not raise it"
				continue
			fi
			# Ran the writer and the air did not change: the fronthaul
			# the controller advertises is not what this box ended up
			# broadcasting, and nothing here will fix it by repeating.
			# Say so once instead of running genconfig every INTERVAL.
			state failed
			# Say it once per value, not once every INTERVAL. The marker holds
			# the credential it was said about, so a later rename is complained
			# about again and the same one is not. An earlier version cleared
			# the marker whenever the value differed from the fuse - which is
			# every pass while it is failing, so "once" never held and the log
			# filled up: measured 2026-08-25, the same line every 30 s for as
			# long as it was watched.
			[ "$want" = "$(cat /etc/mapc/fh-finish.loud 2>/dev/null)" ] || {
				printf '%s\n' "$want" > /etc/mapc/fh-finish.loud
				log "controller advertises '$fh' but this box still broadcasts '${before%%|*}' - genconfig did not move it"
			}
			continue
		fi
		# "settled" means the join is over, and for a node that never got a
		# fronthaul it is a lie.
		#
		# The finisher above only runs when the controller has actually named
		# a fronthaul. When $fh is empty nothing here fires and the node used
		# to fall straight through to settled - so a box waiting for
		# credentials that were never sent reported itself finished. That is
		# how the endless wait for the third restart went unnoticed on
		# 2026-08-19 until somebody restarted the controller by hand and the
		# join completed: the agent was never stuck, it was patiently done.
		#
		# The state file has three meanings for the wizard - keep waiting,
		# failed, finished - and this is "keep waiting", so it stays "working"
		# and the page goes on spinning until its own give-up fires. The
		# precision goes next to it rather than into it: a reason file for
		# anything that wants to know WHAT is missing, and one line in the log
		# so the answer is in the record instead of in somebody's memory.
		if [ -z "$fh" ]; then
			state working
			echo "fronthaul" > /etc/mapc/adopt-waiting 2>/dev/null
			[ -f /etc/mapc/adopt-waiting.loud ] || {
				: > /etc/mapc/adopt-waiting.loud
				log "backhaul stands, but the controller has named no fronthaul yet - still waiting, not settled"
			}
			continue
		fi
		rm -f /etc/mapc/adopt-waiting /etc/mapc/adopt-waiting.loud 2>/dev/null

		# The mesh address, asked for rather than typed in.
		#
		# A button join carries no address - not carrying one is its whole
		# point - so the worker calls easymesh-role without --mesh-addr and
		# the node keeps the address the image ships with. Measured
		# 2026-08-22: an agent joined by button, carried client traffic
		# perfectly, and was invisible from the mesh, because 192.168.1.1 is
		# not in it. It also answered to the same address as the controller's
		# service port, so two boxes in one mesh had one address.
		#
		# Asking the controller directly is not open to us: reaching it needs
		# an address in its network, which is the thing we do not have. DHCP
		# has no such problem - it is broadcast, it works before any address
		# exists, and the controller answering it is the same node that would
		# have answered the question anyway. It also already knows which
		# addresses are free, which is the hard half of the problem.
		#
		# Done here because this is the point where the backhaul stands and
		# the controller is reachable, and the restart it costs replaces the
		# one this loop was going to spend anyway. The fuse keeps it a
		# finisher rather than something that runs every interval.
		# Not before the controller's own credentials (WSC M2) have arrived.
		# The step below runs easymesh-role, whose forget_baked_credentials
		# keeps the keys only when /etc/multiap/wsc_m2.json exists - without
		# it the WPS-delivered backhaul key reads as a factory one: keys
		# wiped, radios disabled, adopt-state gone, node off the mesh for
		# good. Measured 2026-09-19: the controller offered an address 31 s
		# after adopt, before M2. A reboot in between usually hid the race.
		if [ ! -f /etc/mapc/addr-finish.done ] &&
		   [ -f /etc/multiap/wsc_m2.json ] &&
		   [ "$(uci -q get network.lan.proto)" = "static" ] &&
		   [ -z "$(uci -q get network.lan.gateway)" ]; then
			mkdir -p /etc/mapc
			# The fuse is burnt where the work SUCCEEDS, further down,
			# not here.
			#
			# It used to be burnt at the top, on the reasoning that the
			# success path ends in a reboot and must not repeat. True of
			# the success path, and wrong for every other one: the probe
			# below returns nothing whenever the backhaul is not up yet,
			# which is exactly what an early boot looks like. The fuse
			# was then already gone, so the step never ran again and the
			# box kept its factory 192.168.1.1 for good - with a working
			# backhaul, a controller that answered, and no way left to
			# ask it anything.
			#
			# Found 2026-09-04 on a box that had joined properly:
			# addr-finish.done present from a boot at 09:14, and
			# addr-finish.log - written only on success - missing
			# entirely. The marker recorded that we had tried, when the
			# only thing worth recording is that we got somewhere.
			#
			# Retrying costs one DHCP probe per 30 s interval, and only
			# while this node still has no mesh address: taking one
			# clears the condition above.

			# The bridge the mesh lives on, asked rather than assumed - a
			# node that keeps the mesh on a bridge of its own would send the
			# request down the wrong wire and never hear an answer.
			br=$(uci -q get network.easymesh.device)
			[ -n "$br" ] || br=$(uci -q get network.lan.device)
			[ -n "$br" ] || br=br-lan

			cat > /tmp/addr-probe.sh <<'PROBE'
#!/bin/sh
[ "$1" = bound ] || [ "$1" = renew ] || exit 0
printf '%s %s\n' "$ip" "$router" > /tmp/addr-probe.out
PROBE
			chmod +x /tmp/addr-probe.sh
			rm -f /tmp/addr-probe.out
			# -n give up rather than wait forever, -q quit once bound.
			# Nothing is applied by this: the probe script only records.
			udhcpc -i "$br" -n -q -t 4 -T 3 \
				-s /tmp/addr-probe.sh >/dev/null 2>&1

			addr=$(cut -d' ' -f1 /tmp/addr-probe.out 2>/dev/null)
			gw=$(cut -d' ' -f2 /tmp/addr-probe.out 2>/dev/null)
			rm -f /tmp/addr-probe.sh /tmp/addr-probe.out

			case "$addr" in
			''|*[!0-9.]*)
				log "no address offered on $br - staying where we are"
				;;
			*)
				# The offer told us the one thing only DHCP could: which network
				# the mesh is on. It is not where this node should live. The
				# answer comes out of the client pool - measured 2026-08-23, .170
				# out of .100-.249, leased for 12 h - and a node that pins itself
				# there statically holds an address dnsmasq hands to a phone as
				# soon as the lease lapses. The clash would surface a day later,
				# somewhere else, and read as a wireless fault.
				#
				# So the offer is used as a way in, and the place to live is
				# picked from the low end - .2 upwards, which is also the number
				# the controller shows next to "Pair a new box".
				# Ask the controller, which is the only box that keeps a
				# record of what it has already promised.
				#
				# Picking locally cannot be made safe, however well this box
				# probes: between the pick and the moment easymesh-role
				# applies it, every probe anywhere still reports the number as
				# free. On 2026-09-04 two boxes were configured minutes apart,
				# with reboots in between, and both ended up on .2 - the owner
				# waited, the network answered honestly, and the answer was
				# stale by the time it mattered. Only a written promise fixes
				# that, and only the controller can hold the pen.
				#
				# The DHCP offer above is what makes the question askable: it
				# is an address on the controller's network, which is exactly
				# what this box lacked. The controller sits at .1 of it by the
				# same convention the whole product uses.
				alm=$(uci -q get ieee1905.ieee1905.macaddress 2>/dev/null | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')
				[ -n "$alm" ] && [ "$alm" != "00:00:00:00:00:00" ] ||
					alm=$(cat "/sys/class/net/$br/address" 2>/dev/null | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')

				# Knowing the offer is not the same as being able to use it.
				#
				# The probe above runs udhcpc with -n -q and a script that
				# only records, so nothing is configured: the box learns the
				# mesh is on 172.16.20.0/24 and still has no address on it.
				# The request below then has no route to the controller, and
				# the code fell to "the controller did not answer - picking
				# locally, which nothing writes down" every single time.
				# Measured 2026-09-04: the ledger held a reservation the
				# joining node never collected, the node picked its own
				# number, and the two matched only by luck. Called by hand a
				# minute later, from the same box once it HAD an address, the
				# claim went through and returned source "window".
				#
				# So the offered address is held on the bridge for the length
				# of one question and taken off again. Nothing is committed to
				# uci, the box still lives where the controller tells it to,
				# and the address is the one the mesh's own DHCP just said was
				# free - so borrowing it for a few seconds collides with
				# nobody.
				#
				# Deliberately NO trap here: this script already has one on
				# EXIT for the wps_credentials listener, and a second would
				# silently replace it - trading a borrowed address for an
				# orphaned ubus process. The only path out of the block that
				# does not reach the removal below ends in reboot, which takes
				# the address with it.
				_borrowed=""
				if [ -n "$addr" ] && ! ip -4 addr show "$br" 2>/dev/null |
						grep -q "inet ${addr%%/*}/"; then
					ip addr add "$addr/24" dev "$br" 2>/dev/null && _borrowed="$addr"
				fi
				node=$(ask_controller_addr "${addr%.*}.1" "$alm" 2>/dev/null)
				[ -n "$_borrowed" ] && {
					ip addr del "$_borrowed/24" dev "$br" 2>/dev/null
					_borrowed=""
				}

				if [ -n "$node" ]; then
					log "the controller reserved $node for $alm"
				else
					log "the controller did not answer - picking locally, which nothing writes down"

					# pick_mesh_addr vetoes on what 1905 reports rather than
					# on ARP silence, and this box has only just become a 1905
					# member - the view it reads is the one thing here that is
					# not ready the instant the backhaul is. Measured
					# 2026-09-04: an agent at depth 3 sees the full five-node
					# topology with every address, which is exactly what the
					# ARP walk could not see. Wait a bounded moment for it,
					# and say so if it never fills.
					t1905_try=0; t1905_seen=0
					while [ $t1905_try -lt 5 ]; do
						t1905_seen=$(ubus -t 5 call ieee1905.topology dump 2>/dev/null |
							sed -n 's/^[[:space:]]*"ip":[[:space:]]*"\([0-9][0-9.]*\)".*/\1/p' |
							grep -c "^${addr%.*}\.")
						[ "${t1905_seen:-0}" -gt 0 ] && break
						t1905_try=$((t1905_try + 1))
						sleep 2
					done
					if [ "${t1905_seen:-0}" -le 0 ]; then
						# Nothing corroborates this subnet: nobody answered
						# at .1 of it, and no 1905 member lives in it. That
						# is not a slow mesh, it is somebody else's DHCP
						# server - measured 2026-09-21, a foreign agent
						# handing out 192.168.50.x on our own bridge, with
						# 192.168.50.1 answering nothing. Picking from ARP
						# inside it would settle this box in the stranger's
						# network and keep asking the stranger for more.
						#
						# Dropped, not worked around: the fuse below is not
						# burnt on this path, so the next interval asks
						# again - and once the real server answers, or 1905
						# fills, the same offer is accepted normally.
						log "nothing in ${addr%.*}.0/24 belongs to this mesh - the offer of $addr came from somewhere else, ignoring it"
						addr=""
					else
						node=$(pick_mesh_addr "$addr" "$br" 2>/dev/null)
					fi
				fi
				if [ -n "$addr" ] && [ -z "$node" ]; then
					# Better a node inside the pool than a node with no address
					# at all - but say so, because it is a lease waiting to lapse.
					log "no free node address under ${addr%.*}.2-99 - keeping the offered $addr, which is inside the client pool"
					node="$addr"
				fi
				if [ -n "$addr" ] && [ -x /usr/sbin/easymesh-role ]; then
					log "mesh offered $addr (gateway ${gw:-none}) - taking $node, one restart to apply"
					# And again somewhere that survives the reboot this line is
					# about. logger works - a test line reached /var/log/messages
					# in under two seconds - but on 2026-08-23 the real one never
					# landed: the box goes down moments later and rsyslog had not
					# reached the disk. The single line that proves this ran is
					# exactly the line that cannot afford to be lost, so it also
					# goes next to the fuse, on the rootfs, appended not truncated.
					printf '%s mesh offered %s, taking %s (gateway %s)\n' \
						"$(date -Is 2>/dev/null || date)" \
						"$addr" "$node" "${gw:-none}" >> /etc/mapc/addr-finish.log 2>/dev/null
					# Burn the fuse HERE - after an address has been
					# decided and before the reboot that applies it.
					# This is the one moment where "done" is true.
					: > /etc/mapc/addr-finish.done
					state working
					/usr/sbin/easymesh-role agent "${gw:-}" --mesh-addr "$node" \
						>/dev/null 2>&1
					restart_to_apply "mesh address $node applied"
					continue
				fi
				;;
			esac
		fi

		# Only on the way IN to settled: state() is reached every interval
		# once the join is over, and the check costs 20 s of looking.
		#
		# And "settled" is the check's to withhold. Measured 2026-09-20 with
		# a forced fault: the check set `failed`, this line set `settled` one
		# instruction later, and a node the fallback reboot had failed to
		# repair reported itself finished - with the truth sitting in the
		# fuse right next to it.
		if [ "$(cat $STATE 2>/dev/null)" = settled ]; then
			# A settled node is not joining, whatever a leftover
			# stamp says. This is the only line a box that leaked
			# one before the fix above existed will ever reach, so
			# the repair for the installed base belongs here.
			rm -f "$JOIN_STARTED"
			state settled
			# The round after the clean reboot lands here: say how it went.
			join_clean_outcome
		elif join_final_check "settled"; then
			state settled
			join_clean_reboot
		fi
		continue
	fi

	adopt "$key"
done
